title Mycelium Pledge r1 — a two-phase transfer decided once by the order (the equivocation crux)
prompt remember/loop card: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve own design rounds and make progress before checking in; double-seat visions; send each finished increment as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think Read REMEMBER + both itinerary docs, then reconciled against git — the card's git nib (65615748cd) is ~40 commits stale; the live edge is the Mycelium consensus arc (Season D). The Purse arc closed at 20260813.074353 (r4, app == awk). Three contested-verdict ledgers now stand (Tenure names · Till global supply · Purse per-account balances), each a 4-round arc over the Cord's agreed order, editing none of cord/fold/kumara.
think Chose the next crux Lindy-first crux-first. The prior log's recommend named .kres (a cairn-gated breach rename — opens no new capability) or Puddle (a jump off the productive ledger spine). Both are lower-Lindy than the ledger's own next capability. Every ledger so far settles a transfer in ONE committed fact; the truest thing a value ledger owes a real trade is atomicity across TWO steps — reserve, then post-or-void — which is TigerBeetle's signature two-phase transfer and exactly what the double-seat expansion names ("improving on TigerBeetle and Mysticeti"). That is the hardest still-tractable crux on the whole Mycelium road, so it wins.
think Confirmed the seam edits nothing: fold.verify_fact trusts a fact by its signature over kind·amount·star·body WITHOUT restricting the kind, so a new law can fold over kind_tax facts and read the phase from a body tag bound into the signature — exactly as the Purse read its recipient from the body. Phase tags P·id·to (reserve) / H·id (post) / V·id (void); a post/void carries the pledge's own positive amount (so mint_fact accepts a kind_tax) and that signed amount must equal the referenced pledge's amount, binding phase, id, and sum together.
think The crux is equivocation: a holder signs BOTH a post and a void for one pledge at the same round (the twin of the Purse's double-spender). The Cord's total order is deterministic regardless of arrival, so the FIRST resolution the order places decides and the second is a lawful no-op. Conservation is the Purse's law widened by one column: balance + reserved always sums to exactly what genesis issued (pledge moves balance→reserved, post reserved→recipient, void reserved→balance).
obs One correction on metal: cord.add enforces parent-before-child (DanglingParent), so a fully-reversed arrival permutation is not an addable DAG. The genuine reordering freedom is between the two equivocating round-2 leaves (both children of the pledge block); resolving both their orders (base and the swap) to one identical verdict is the honest invariance proof, mirroring the Purse's resolve_perm. Dropped the invalid reverse permutation.
obs Selftest covers the whole shape: the equivocation decided exactly once and identically across arrival; a clean honor (Bob +100, Alice 0/0); a clean release (Alice 100 back, reserved 0); an open pledge holding coins reserved-not-spendable; and four refusals — unknown pledge id, wrong signer settling another's pledge, amount mismatch, and a star reserve — each refusing whole. GREEN first pass; conservation asserted at every case.
